Introduction
Planning can defined as the process of developing a comprehensive and individualized care plan for a patient based on their assessed needs, goals, and available resources (Doenges, 2016). It involves setting priorities, establishing interventions, and creating a roadmap for delivering patient-centered care.
Objective/Outcome Criteria
In order to ensure the delivery of appropriate interventions and wholesome achievement of the desired goal or outcome of the measures directed to improve patient condition, nursing objectives plus their respective criteria were set to be achieved and to measure the effectiveness of the interventions implemented. Here are all the nursing objectives formulated and their corresponding criteria;
Patient will attain optimum lung ventilation within 72hours as evidenced by;
Patient stating  the use of less effort in breathing
Nurse recording normal oxygen saturation level (95%-100%)
Patient would be relieved of chest pain within 24 hours as evidenced by;
Patient rating pain less than 4 on the pain Wong-Baker faces scales
Patient lying comfortably and relaxed in bed
Patient would attain an optimal body temperature of (36.2°C to 37.2°C) within 48hours as evidenced by;
Patient verbalizing absence of body hotness and shivering
Nurse recording an optimal temperature of  (36.2°C to 37.2°C)
Patient would maintain optimal tissue perfusion throughout the duration of hospitalization as evidenced by;
Patient having a normal capillary refill time of less than 3 seconds
Nurse observation patient without digital clubbing
Patient would attain a plausible amount of strength and be able to perform activities of daily living within 72 hours as evidenced by;
Nurse observing patient appear clean and tidy
Patient verbalizing the use of less effort in performing basic activities
Patient would maintain optimum hydration status throughout the duration of hospitalization as evidenced by;
Patient having good skin turgor
Patient having moist mucous membranes
